# Services

## Company Formation
URL: https://alanfaldoc.com/services/company-formation
Author: Al Anfal Doc Team. Last updated: 2026-11-05.

**Quick answer:** Company formation in the UAE means registering a legal business entity either on the mainland or in a free zone. Most business activities now allow 100% foreign ownership. The process covers choosing a jurisdiction and legal structure, reserving a trade name, obtaining approvals, signing the incorporation documents and receiving the trade licence.

Key takeaways:
- Mainland and free zone are the two main routes, each with different trading rights
- Most activities allow 100% foreign ownership
- The trade licence is issued after name approval and incorporation documents
- Residence visas and a corporate bank account normally follow the licence

At a glance:
- Who it is for: Founders, investors and foreign companies
- Main options: Mainland, free zone, offshore
- Typical steps: Name reservation, approvals, licence, visas, bank account
- Timeline: Depends on jurisdiction and activity. We confirm it after reviewing your file

FAQs:
Q: Can a foreigner own 100% of a company in the UAE?
A: In most business activities, yes. Ownership rules depend on your activity and jurisdiction (mainland or free zone). We confirm eligibility for your specific activity before you commit.
Q: What is the difference between a mainland and a free zone company?
A: A mainland licence lets you trade across the UAE and with government entities, while a free zone licence offers a simpler setup and is usually limited to trading within the zone or internationally. We compare both against your business plan.
Q: How long does company formation take?
A: Timelines depend on the jurisdiction, the activity and how quickly documents are provided. After reviewing your file we give you a realistic timeline.
Q: Do I need a physical office?
A: Most licences require registered office space, but the type (flexi-desk, shared or dedicated) depends on the jurisdiction and activity. We advise on the lowest-cost compliant option.

Sources:
- UAE Government Portal (u.ae): https://u.ae/en

## PRO Services
URL: https://alanfaldoc.com/services/pro-services
Author: Al Anfal Doc Team. Last updated: 2026-11-05.

**Quick answer:** PRO services are professional government-relations services. A Public Relations Officer submits and tracks your paperwork with UAE authorities, including visas, labour cards, licence renewals, approvals and attestations, so you avoid queues and processing mistakes. They suit companies that want compliance handled by a dedicated team.

Key takeaways:
- Handles visas, labour cards, renewals and approvals on your behalf
- Reduces delays caused by incomplete or incorrect paperwork
- Renewal dates are tracked so licences and visas never lapse
- Works across immigration, labour, free zone and municipal departments

At a glance:
- Who it is for: Companies and individuals with ongoing government paperwork
- Typical tasks: Employee visas, labour cards, licence renewals, attestations
- Departments: Immigration, labour, free zones, municipalities, chambers
- Format: One-off transactions or an ongoing PRO arrangement

FAQs:
Q: What does a PRO service do?
A: A PRO (Public Relations Officer) handles government paperwork on your behalf: visas, labour cards, licence renewals, approvals and attestations, so you do not have to queue at government offices.
Q: Which government departments do you deal with?
A: We handle transactions with immigration, labour, free zone authorities, municipalities, chambers of commerce and other UAE entities.
Q: Can you manage renewals for me?
A: Yes. We track expiry dates for licences, visas and cards and renew them ahead of time to avoid fines.

Sources:
- UAE Government Portal (u.ae): https://u.ae/en
- Ministry of Human Resources & Emiratisation (MOHRE): https://www.mohre.gov.ae

## Visa Processing
URL: https://alanfaldoc.com/services/visa-processing
Author: Al Anfal Doc Team. Last updated: 2026-11-05.

**Quick answer:** Visa processing in the UAE covers employment, investor, family, tourist and golden visa applications. Each visa type has its own eligibility rules and documents, and decisions are made only by the authorities. A complete, correctly attested file reduces delays and avoidable rejections, which is where a professional service helps most.

Key takeaways:
- Employment, investor, family, tourist and golden visas are the main categories
- Approval is decided by the authorities, never guaranteed by a service provider
- A complete, attested document set is the best way to avoid delays
- Renewals and cancellations should be tracked ahead of expiry

At a glance:
- Visa types: Employment, investor, family, tourist, golden
- Typical documents: Passport, photo, sponsor or company file, medical fitness result
- Decision made by: UAE immigration authorities
- Timeline: Varies by visa type and authority

FAQs:
Q: Which visas can you help with?
A: Employment, investor, family sponsorship, tourist and golden visa applications, as well as renewals and cancellations.
Q: Do you guarantee a visa approval?
A: No. Decisions are made by the authorities. What we do is prepare a complete, correct application, which reduces delays and avoidable rejections.
Q: How long does a visa application take?
A: Processing time depends on the visa type and the authority. We give you an estimate after reviewing your documents and keep you updated until a decision.
Q: What documents are usually required?
A: A valid passport, photo and supporting documents that vary by visa type, such as a sponsor or company file and a medical fitness result. We send you a checklist for your case.

Sources:
- UAE Government Portal (u.ae): https://u.ae/en
- Federal Authority for Identity, Citizenship, Customs & Port Security: https://icp.gov.ae

## Document Clearing
URL: https://alanfaldoc.com/services/document-clearing
Author: Al Anfal Doc Team. Last updated: 2026-11-05.

**Quick answer:** Document clearing, also called attestation or legalisation, is the official verification of a document so authorities in another country accept it. Documents usually pass through the issuing country's authorities, the UAE embassy and the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs. Each step has its own requirements, so the order and completeness of the chain matter.

Key takeaways:
- Attestation makes foreign documents valid for official use in the UAE
- The usual chain: issuing authority, foreign ministry, UAE embassy, UAE MOFA
- Educational, personal and commercial documents all follow similar steps
- Express options exist for many document types

At a glance:
- Common documents: Degree certificates, marriage and birth certificates, powers of attorney, commercial agreements
- Typical chain: Issuing authority, foreign ministry, UAE embassy, UAE MOFA
- Often needed with: Certified translation
- Timeline: Depends on the document and the authority

FAQs:
Q: What is document attestation?
A: Attestation is the official verification of a document by the relevant authorities so it is accepted in another country, for example certificates, contracts or powers of attorney for use in the UAE.
Q: Which documents can you clear?
A: Educational, personal (marriage, birth) and commercial documents, powers of attorney and agreements. Contact us with your document and destination and we will confirm the steps.
Q: Do you offer express service?
A: Express options are available for many document types. Availability and timing depend on the authority involved.

Sources:
- UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs: https://www.mofa.gov.ae
- UAE Government Portal (u.ae): https://u.ae/en

## Trade License
URL: https://alanfaldoc.com/services/trade-license
Author: Al Anfal Doc Team. Last updated: 2026-11-05.

**Quick answer:** A trade licence is the official permit that allows a business to operate in a UAE emirate. The main categories are commercial, professional, industrial and tourism licences, and the right one depends on your business activity. Licences must be renewed every year before expiry to avoid fines and keep related visas valid.

Key takeaways:
- Commercial, professional, industrial and tourism are the main licence types
- The correct licence depends on your business activity
- Renew before the expiry date to avoid fines
- Activities can often be added later through an amendment

At a glance:
- Licence types: Commercial, professional, industrial, tourism
- Validity: Renewed annually
- Common changes: Add or remove activities, change address or shareholders
- Issued by: The licensing authority of your emirate or free zone

FAQs:
Q: What types of trade licence are there?
A: Commercial, professional, industrial and tourism licences are the main categories. The right one depends on your business activity.
Q: When should I renew my trade licence?
A: Renew before the expiry date to avoid fines. We send reminders and handle the renewal for you.
Q: Can I add a new activity to my licence?
A: In many cases yes, through an amendment. Approvals and fees depend on the authority. We check feasibility first.

Sources:
- UAE Government Portal (u.ae): https://u.ae/en

## Legal Translation
URL: https://alanfaldoc.com/services/legal-translation
Author: Al Anfal Doc Team. Last updated: 2026-11-05.

**Quick answer:** A legal translation is a certified rendering of a document by an approved translator, accepted by courts and government bodies. In the UAE, Arabic and English are the most common pairs. Translations are often combined with notarisation or attestation so the document can be used officially.

Key takeaways:
- Certified translators stamp the translation as accurate
- Arabic and English are the most requested language pairs
- Often combined with notarisation or attestation
- Used for contracts, court papers, company documents and certificates

At a glance:
- Common languages: Arabic, English, other languages on request
- Typical documents: Contracts, court documents, incorporation papers, certificates
- Often combined with: Notarisation and attestation
- Accepted by: Courts, ministries, embassies and licensing authorities

FAQs:
Q: What is a certified legal translation?
A: A translation prepared by an approved translator and stamped as an accurate rendering, accepted by courts and government bodies.
Q: Which languages do you translate?
A: Arabic and English are the most common pairs, with other languages available on request.
Q: Can you translate and attest in one go?
A: Yes. We can handle the translation and the follow-on notarisation or attestation as one service.

Sources:
- UAE Government Portal (u.ae): https://u.ae/en
- UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs: https://www.mofa.gov.ae

## Bank Account Opening
URL: https://alanfaldoc.com/services/bank-account-opening
Author: Al Anfal Doc Team. Last updated: 2026-11-05.

**Quick answer:** Opening a corporate bank account in the UAE requires a complete application and passing the bank's own compliance checks. Banks typically ask for the trade licence, incorporation documents, shareholder and manager IDs, proof of address and a business profile. Approval is the bank's decision, so a clear, complete file improves your chances.

Key takeaways:
- Banks make their own compliance decisions, so approval is never guaranteed
- A complete application with a clear business profile helps
- Requirements differ by bank and by business type
- Some banks allow part of the process remotely

At a glance:
- Typical documents: Trade licence, memorandum of association, shareholder IDs, proof of address, business profile
- Decision made by: The bank's compliance team
- Our role: Prepare the file and introduce suitable banks
- Timeline: Varies by bank and profile

FAQs:
Q: Can you guarantee my bank account will be approved?
A: No. Banks make their own compliance decisions. We prepare a complete application and introduce you to banks that fit your profile to improve your chances.
Q: What documents are needed to open a corporate account?
A: Typically the trade licence, memorandum of association, shareholder and manager IDs, proof of address and a business profile. Requirements vary by bank.
Q: Do I need to be in the UAE to open an account?
A: Some banks require an in-person visit and others support remote steps. We confirm the process for your chosen bank.

Sources:
- Central Bank of the UAE: https://www.centralbank.ae

